<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mentuhotep II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2055–2004 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Nebhepetre Mentuhotep</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mentuhotep II was a king of the Theban Eleventh Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Around 2055 BCE he defeated the rival Herakleopolitan kings and reunited the country after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, an achievement usually taken as the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>. His terraced mortuary temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, later inspired the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> beside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii.jpg" alt="Mentuhotep II" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Montuhotep_from_Deir_al-Bahri,_11th_Dynasty,_2065-2014_BCE;_Egyptian_Museum,_Cairo_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
